<p><a id=3D"chapter-03"></a></p>
<h2>03 =E2=80=94 Record useful pieces and build the voice track first</h2>
<p><strong>Your result:</strong> the finished narration for one short expla=
iner, plus clean screen clips. You should already have the script, storyboa=
rd and an editor that successfully exports a test.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Make the audio the structural backbone</h3>
<p>An audio-first edit lets you solve the argument and delivery before spen=
ding time animating it. Record one or two sentences at a time. Leave a smal=
l margin before and after each take. If you stumble, pause and repeat the w=
hole phrase with the same energy rather than restarting the entire video. L=
ater you can choose the better take without rebuilding everything around it=
.</p>
<p>Use a slightly more deliberate delivery than a casual conversation, whil=
e keeping your normal voice. Emphasize the important word, not every word. =
=E2=80=9CThe JSON is valid. The answer is wrong.=E2=80=9D needs a small sep=
aration so the contradiction lands. Increasing playback speed changes timbr=
e and comprehension; perform the line at the desired energy instead.</p>
<p>Keep a consistent mouth-to-microphone relationship. Start around 10=E2=
=80=9315 cm for a suitable close-talk mic, test the manufacturer's guidance=
, then adjust for plosives and tone. Do not assume all microphones accept t=
he same distance or speak into the same side. Aim slightly away from the br=
eath stream and use a windscreen or pop filter where needed. <a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.shure.com/en-US/docs/education/Microphone-Techniques-for-Recording=
">Shure recording education</a> explains placement as an important part of =
the recording chain.</p>
<p>Set gain with the loudest sentence you expect to say. An initial peak ra=
nge around =E2=88=9212 to =E2=88=926 dBFS gives room for variation; it is a=
 practical starting point, not a loudness standard or a description of Jeff=
's settings. Avoid hitting 0 dBFS. Record a few seconds of room sound so ab=
rupt changes in background noise become easier to notice during the edit.</=
p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Record with the simplest reliable route</h3>
<p>You may record directly into a mono dialogue track in your editor, or re=
cord a microphone-only scene in OBS and use that audio. Direct editor recor=
ding is convenient after you have tested input routing. OBS is useful when =
you want a consistent source setup. Disable duplicate input capture so your=
 microphone is not accidentally recorded twice with a short delay.</p>
<p>Follow the <a href=3D"https://obsproject.com/kb/quick-start-guide">OBS Q=
uick Start</a> for scene, source and audio setup. Use <a href=3D"https://ob=
sproject.com/kb/hybrid-mp4">Hybrid MP4</a> with compatible H.264/AAC if you=
r short import test succeeds; MKV plus remux is a useful fallback. These ar=
e current container choices, not an instruction to change an already reliab=
le workflow. Record and import a thirty-second sample before a large sessio=
n.</p>
<p>Create a dedicated browser profile with synthetic data and hide notifica=
tions. For a 1920=C3=971080 screen recording, enlarge the editor font until=
 the important line is readable in a small preview. Start around 24=E2=80=
=9332 pixels and judge the actual export; different fonts and zoom levels v=
ary. Show the relevant pane, not a whole desktop full of tiny text.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Assemble the narration</h3>
<p>Place the chosen takes on the dialogue track in story order. Remove mist=
akes, repeated starts and pauses that do no work. Listen without visuals. D=
oes the argument make sense? Are any edits swallowing consonants? Are you f=
orcing the viewer to process two difficult ideas without a breath? Repair t=
hose issues before adding motion.</p>
<p>Use short fades or tiny crossfades where a cut produces an audible click=
. A click often comes from a waveform discontinuity; covering every edit wi=
th a large dissolve is unnecessary. Preserve natural breaths when they supp=
ort cadence. Remove a distracting breath individually rather than applying =
aggressive gating that makes every word sound isolated.</p>
<p>Balance phrase levels with clip gain first. Compression can reduce exces=
sive dynamic variation; it cannot undo clipped audio. A de-esser can reduce=
 harsh sibilance; it should not turn speech into a lisp. Equalization shoul=
d address a heard problem, such as muddiness, rather than copy someone else=
's numerical preset. Compare processed and raw audio at similar perceived v=
olume so louder does not automatically seem better.</p>
<p>Adobe users can tag clips as Dialogue and use the documented <a href=3D"=
https://helpx.adobe.com/uk/premiere/desktop/add-audio-effects/adjust-volume=
-and-levels/audio-editing-with-essential-sound-panel.html">Essential Sound =
controls</a>. <a href=3D"https://helpx.adobe.com/in/premiere/desktop/add-au=
dio-effects/adjust-volume-and-levels/auto-match-audio-loudness.html">Auto-M=
atch</a> helps align clips, but its displayed target is not a universal You=
Tube setting. Resolve users should use the <a href=3D"https://www.blackmagi=
cdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/training">official Fairlight learning s=
ections</a> for Recording VO and ADR and introductory audio, applying only =
what the current task needs.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>Capture code as prepared visual states</h3>
<p>Finish and test the example before recording the teaching footage. Captu=
re the initial result, the input, the relevant check and the final output a=
s separate clips. Each clip needs a second or two of stillness before and a=
fter its important action. That gives you clean editing handles.</p>
<p>One useful reconstruction method is to remove prepared code in reverse t=
eaching order, begin recording, then useUndo to reveal those blocks in forw=
ard order. Another is to record deliberate pastes. Keep the original code s=
afe and verify the restored example afterward. This is an edited explanatio=
n, not a speed-coding demonstration; do not suggest the implementation was =
written instantly or generated by a tool if it was not.</p>
<p>When the narration needs longer than the screen action, hold a clean fra=
me rather than repeatedly slowing the mouse movement. A viewer should not w=
atch your cursor drift merely because the sentence is unfinished. Lesson 4 =
shows how to build holds and highlights. For your optional face segment, re=
cord one short orientation or closing sentence at eye level with a window o=
r soft lamp and the same consistent audio.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>This week's practice</h3>
<p>Monday 60 min: test placement, levels and input routing. Tuesday 75 min:=
 record the script in short phrases. Wednesday 60 min: select takes and ass=
emble dialogue. Thursday 75 min: repair timing and level differences. Frida=
y 30 min: listen on headphones and ordinary speakers. Saturday 150 min: cap=
ture the prepared code/output states and optional camera sentence. Sunday 6=
0 min: export an audio-led rough version and mark every confusing transitio=
n.</p>
<p>Done means the narration works by itself, no phrase clips or vanishes at=
 an edit, and every essential screen action exists in a usable clip. That i=
s the foundation for energetic editing. Effects cannot substitute for it.</=
p>
