<p><a id=3D"chapter-05"></a></p>
<h2>05 =E2=80=94 Assemble one complete video and finish it carefully</h2>
<p><strong>Your result:</strong> a complete original 90=E2=80=93150-second =
developer explainer. You have edited narration, tested screen evidence and =
seven reusable moves. This week combines them in a sensible order.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Pass 1: the audio-led assembly</h3>
<p>Duplicate your template and save a new project. Put the completed dialog=
ue on its own track. Add markers for the hook, explanation, demonstration, =
limitation and payoff. Do not change the argument every time you discover a=
n attractive animation. If the argument is still wrong, return to the scrip=
t before polishing.</p>
<p>Place the main evidence shots first: the incorrect result, successful pa=
rse, failed arithmetic check and corrected example. These are the video's l=
oad-bearing images. Then fill the transitions with simple code states or di=
agrams. Only after the lesson works should you add keyword pops and a joke.=
 This order protects the explanation from being buried under decoration.</p=
>
<p>Use separate tracks for evidence, graphics and titles where your editor =
allows. Name tracks or clips so you can find the right material. Keep music=
 and sound effects separate from dialogue. If a title accidentally obscures=
 a result, you should be able to move it without rebuilding the recording.<=
/p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Pass 2: timing and comprehension</h3>
<p>Replay each sentence and ask what the viewer must inspect at that moment=
. If the sentence names an object not yet visible, adjust the shot. If the =
important code changes before it can be read, extend a hold. If the diagram=
 stays after its explanatory job ends, shorten it. The desired speed follow=
s comprehension.</p>
<p>You can use a few two-to-five-second visual beats as a starting exercise=
, with longer holds for code and diagrams. This is your practice range, not=
 a measured average or a rule for every video. A new label can supply a bea=
t without a full cut. Meaningful variation is more useful than a fixed time=
r.</p>
<p>Make the first fifteen seconds especially concrete. Show the contradicti=
on, identify the topic and establish that you have evidence. Do not spend t=
hat time listing the channel's future promises. The ending should give the =
answer the opening implied: parsing checks format; correctness requires a t=
est for the task. A brief next step may follow, but the lesson should alrea=
dy be complete.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Pass 3: sound design</h3>
<p>Begin with dialogue that is comfortable without music. Then add backgrou=
nd music only if it improves the experience. Use a track with suitable righ=
ts, such as one chosen under the <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/yout=
ube/answer/3376882">YouTube Audio Library's actual conditions</a>. Check tr=
ack-specific attribution. A public GIF or popular sound clip does not autom=
atically include permission for your use.</p>
<p>Lower music until every technical word remains effortless to understand =
on ordinary speakers. Different recordings have different loudness, so =E2=
=80=9Cmusic at =E2=88=9225 dB=E2=80=9D is not a universal mix recipe. Use v=
olume automation to reduce it during dense explanations or important quiet =
lines. Adobe's current <a href=3D"https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere/desktop/=
add-audio-effects/adjust-volume-and-levels/automatically-duck-audio.html">d=
ucking guidance</a> describes automation, but generated keyframes still nee=
d review.</p>
<p>Use a small sound palette: perhaps a restrained tick for an appearing la=
bel and a quiet impact for the single punchline. Vary silence too. Do not p=
lace a sound on every cut. A sound accent should not force someone to turn =
down the volume after they adjusted it for your voice.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>Pass 4: readable graphics and a finished picture</h3>
<p>Check the video at a small viewing size. Can you read the important outp=
ut? Do you know which line the narration discusses? Do the labels agree wit=
h the code? Does the highlighted color retain meaning throughout? Remove fi=
ne detail that becomes visual noise. A full IDE window may look technical w=
hile communicating less than a tight crop.</p>
<p>For camera footage, correct obvious exposure and white-balance differenc=
es. Your optional face sentence should feel like part of the same video, no=
t a reason to learn a cinematic grading workflow. Use a consistent frame ra=
te and avoid unnecessary switches between bright and dark graphics. Dramati=
c flashes are not required for an energetic technical lesson.</p>
<p>Correct captions for code names and technical terms. Distinguish subtitl=
es from keyword graphics: captions help access the spoken meaning; graphics=
 establish hierarchy. You do not need large animated word-by-word captions =
covering your code. Check both the actual screen text and the caption track=
.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>Export, inspect, then decide whether to publish</h3>
<p>Export a short problematic section first if you are uncertain about fram=
e holds, fonts or audio. Then export the full video. A simple starting choi=
ce is 1920=C3=971080 SDR, the timeline's frame rate, H.264 video and AAC au=
dio at 48 kHz. Refer to <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/youtube/answe=
r/1722171">YouTube's current encoding guidance</a>; its bitrate table is a =
reference, not a quality guarantee for dense screen footage.</p>
<p>Watch the exported file outside the editor, start to finish. Check the b=
eginning, every evidence shot, the last frame and audio tails. If possible,=
 inspect on a phone as well. A clean editor preview does not prove the expo=
rt is correct. Fix missing graphics, clipped sound, unreadable code and ina=
ccurate claims before deciding to upload.</p>
<p>Give yourself an overnight break before the final review when the schedu=
le allows. You are more likely to notice a confusing transition after forge=
tting the sentence you intended to say. Ask one appropriate viewer what the=
y understood the demonstration to establish. Do not coach them toward your =
desired interpretation.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-6"></a></p>
<h3>This week's blocks</h3>
<p>Monday 60 min: audio markers and main evidence. Tuesday 75 min: diagrams=
 and code reveals. Wednesday 60 min: first-fifteen-second revision. Thursda=
y 75 min: restrained motion and one original joke. Friday 30 min: dialogue/=
music balance. Saturday 150 min: captions, export and small-screen inspecti=
on. Sunday 60 min: fresh-eye review and specific repairs. That totals 8.5 h=
ours.</p>
<p>Your result can remain a private practice video. The goal is a complete =
artifact with a coherent promise, accurate proof, comfortable sound and con=
trolled visual rhythm. Publishing quality grows through finished revisions,=
 not indefinitely polishing a project you never watch from beginning to end=
.</p>
