<p><a id=3D"playbook-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Keep the editing order</h3>
<p><strong>Argument =E2=86=92 voice =E2=86=92 evidence =E2=86=92 supporting=
 graphics =E2=86=92 sound =E2=86=92 final inspection.</strong> If the expla=
nation fails at an earlier stage, fix it there. Do not animate around an in=
accurate claim or a missing result.</p>
<p>Use the template you built. Keep dialogue separate from music and effect=
s. Use a small original visual kit: one title style, a keyword card, a code=
 highlight, a result comparison and a three-card diagram. Choose a few usef=
ul moves rather than forcing all seven into every video.</p>
<p><a id=3D"playbook-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>The first fifteen seconds</h3>
<p>Show the actual result or contradiction. Name the problem in plain langu=
age. Make the expected payoff clear. For the sample: a neatly formatted wro=
ng answer, a successful parse and the promise of a separate correctness che=
ck. Skip a long logo intro and a personal r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9. A short camera =
line is optional when it helps connect the lesson to you.</p>
<p><a id=3D"playbook-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>The sound checks</h3>
<ul>
<li>No clipped peaks, swallowed words or accidental double microphone captu=
re.</li>
<li>Phrases have a consistent perceived level.</li>
<li>Pauses are deliberate; consonants and breaths still sound natural.</li>
<li>Music and effects remain subordinate to speech on ordinary speakers.</l=
i>
<li>Processing improves the recording when compared at matched loudness.</l=
i>
</ul>
<p><a id=3D"playbook-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>The picture checks</h3>
<ul>
<li>The currently discussed object is visible.</li>
<li>Code and output are readable in a small preview.</li>
<li>New ideas get useful changes; difficult ideas get enough time.</li>
<li>Important lines have restrained highlights and clear labels.</li>
<li>Actual results are distinguishable from illustrative graphics or staged=
 fixtures.</li>
<li>The joke and graphics are original or appropriately licensed.</li>
</ul>
<p><a id=3D"playbook-section-6"></a></p>
<h3>The final decision</h3>
<p>Watch the exported video all the way through, outside the editor. Fix mi=
ssing assets, factual problems, unreadable evidence and distracting sound b=
efore publishing. A high effects count does not compensate for these failur=
es. Save the script, sources, editable project and final export together.</=
p>
<p>Then record four things: actual hours by stage, one moment that worked, =
one specific weakness and one improvement for the next video. If this tiny =
film took 30 hours, do not promise a weekly five-minute film. Identify the =
slow stage, simplify the next topic and use the same kit again.</p>
<p>Once you can make two clear short explainers, move to 3=E2=80=935 minute=
s when the teaching genuinely needs more reasoning. Reuse the method and ad=
d useful examples. Matching another creator's polish is a direction for pra=
ctice, not a guaranteed deadline.</p>
