<p><a id=3D"sample-script"></a></p>
<h2>Original practice film: =E2=80=9CYour JSON is valid. The answer is stil=
l wrong.=E2=80=9D</h2>
<p>This is an original script for Aadil, not a Fireship transcript or imita=
tion of Jeff's voice. Treat runtime as a rehearsal result. The footage is a=
 synthetic arithmetic demonstration; it is not a benchmark of a particular =
AI model.</p>
<p>The narration below has 184 whitespace-separated words: about 73.6 secon=
ds at 150 words/minute before pauses and screen-reading time. The ninety-se=
cond storyboard therefore leaves roughly sixteen seconds for those holds. R=
ehearse and adjust rather than speeding up to match the table.</p>
<p><a id=3D"sample-script-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Narration</h3>
<p>This answer is valid JSON. It also says two plus two is five. Apparently=
, formatting has solved mathematics.</p>
<p>When you ask a model for structured output, there are different question=
s hiding inside the word=E2=80=9Cvalid.=E2=80=9D Can your application read =
the response? Does it have the fields you expect? And is the answer actuall=
y correct?</p>
<p>Watch this tiny example. The response contains two numbers and a sum. Py=
thon's JSON parser accepts it. Our field check accepts it too. But the arit=
hmetic check compares the returned sum with the inputs=E2=80=94and fails.</=
p>
<p>That's the distinction. Structure tells your program how to read an answ=
er. A task-specific check tells it something about whether that answer is u=
sable.</p>
<p>Here, the check is easy because addition has a definite result. In a res=
earch summary, correctness is harder: you need evidence and evaluation suit=
ed to the claims. A parser cannot decide whether a citation supports a sent=
ence.</p>
<p>So keep the format checks. Then ask one more question: what would a wron=
g answer look like in this particular task, and how could I detect it?</p>
<p>Start there. Beautifully organized nonsense is still nonsense.</p>
